  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes the brainstem and forebrain circuits that regulate pain signals descending to the spinal cord. A pain transmission neuron in the dorsal horn is shown in pink, carrying ascending nociceptive information upward while also being strongly influenced by descending inputs. The central pathway runs through the periaqueductal gray (PAG) and the rostral ventromedial medulla (RVM), which send projections back to the dorsal horn to either suppress or facilitate spinal pain processing.\n\nSeveral higher brain regions appear to feed into this system, including the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC), amygdala, and hypothalamic/thalamic areas. Green and red pathways indicate different functional influences converging on PAG and RVM and then on spinal dorsal horn neurons; visually, these seem to represent opposing control systems, with one pathway dampening pain transmission and another enhancing it. An additional orange pathway in the medulla also contributes to modulation at the spinal level.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes that pain is not transmitted passively from spinal cord to brain. Instead, it is dynamically shaped by emotional, cognitive, and homeostatic centers acting through the PAG–RVM descending control network to increase or decrease nociceptive output.",
